CertHub.register({"id":"cissp","vendor":"ISC2","name":"ISC2 CISSP","short":"CISSP","exam":"2024 outline","blurb":"Management-level security certification across eight domains, from risk and governance to software security.","status":"verified","statusNote":"Weights confirmed against ISC2's CISSP outline on Sept 24, 2026. Certification needs five years of experience (Associate of ISC2 path otherwise).","lastVerified":"2026-09-24","notices":[],"examInfo":{"questions":"100–150 (computerized adaptive testing)","minutes":180,"pass":"700 out of 1000","extra":"Certification needs five years of cumulative paid work experience in two or more of the eight domains (a four-year degree or an approved credential waives one year). Without it, pass the exam to become an Associate of ISC2 and earn the experience within six years."},"examSim":{"questions":150,"minutes":180},"sources":[{"label":"ISC2 CISSP certification exam outline","url":"https://www.isc2.org/certifications/cissp/cissp-certification-exam-outline"}],"planWeeks":20,"domains":[{"id":1,"name":"Security & risk management","w":16,"topics":["Professional ethics: ISC2 Code of Ethics canons and organizational ethics","Security concepts: CIA triad, authenticity, non-repudiation","Security governance: alignment with business strategy, roles, due care vs due diligence","Legal and regulatory issues: cybercrime, privacy law, intellectual property, transborder data flow","Investigation types: administrative, criminal, civil, regulatory","Policies, standards, procedures, baselines and guidelines","Business continuity: BIA, RTO/RPO/MTD, BCP scope","Personnel security: screening, onboarding, transfers, termination, vendor agreements","Risk management: identification, assessment (qualitative and quantitative), response, frameworks","Threat modeling methodologies (STRIDE, PASTA) and supply chain risk management","Security awareness, education and training program effectiveness"],"notes":["Outline 1.1–1.13"],"labs":["Write a one-page business impact analysis for a small business: list three critical processes, their MTD, RTO and RPO, and the dependencies each relies on.","Run a quantitative risk calculation for three assets: estimate asset value, exposure factor and ARO, compute SLE and ALE, then decide whether a proposed $20,000/year control is justified.","Draft a short acceptable use policy, then write one standard, one procedure and one guideline that support it, labeling which is mandatory."]},{"id":2,"name":"Asset security","w":10,"topics":["Identifying and classifying information and assets","Information and asset handling requirements (marking, labeling, storage)","Provisioning resources securely and asset inventory","Data lifecycle: create, store, use, share, archive, destroy","Data roles: owner, controller, processor, custodian, steward, subject","Data collection limitation, location and maintenance","Data retention and end-of-life (EOL/EOS) assets","Data remanence and sanitization: clearing, purging, destruction","Data security controls for data at rest, in transit and in use","Scoping, tailoring and standards selection; DRM, DLP and CASB"],"notes":["Outline 2.1–2.6"],"labs":["Build a four-level data classification scheme for a mid-size company: define each level, give two example data types, and list handling rules for storage, transmission and disposal.","Pick three media types (HDD, SSD, paper) and write the sanitization method you would require for each before reuse and before disposal, citing NIST SP 800-88 categories.","Map one dataset (e.g., customer records) through its full lifecycle and name the data owner, custodian and processor at each stage."]},{"id":3,"name":"Security architecture & engineering","w":13,"topics":["Secure design principles: least privilege, defense in depth, secure defaults, fail securely, zero trust, privacy by design, SASE","Security models: Bell-LaPadula, Biba, Clark-Wilson, Brewer-Nash","Controls based on system security requirements; evaluation criteria (Common Criteria)","Security capabilities of information systems: TPM, memory protection, HSM","Vulnerabilities in architectures: client, server, database, cloud, IoT, ICS/OT, virtualization, containers, serverless","Cryptographic solutions: symmetric, asymmetric, hashing, PKI, key management lifecycle","Cryptanalytic attacks: brute force, side channel, man-in-the-middle, pass the hash, ransomware","Secure site and facility design","Site and facility controls: wiring closets, server rooms, utilities, HVAC, fire suppression, environmental","Information system lifecycle: stakeholder needs through retirement"],"notes":["Outline 3.1–3.10"],"labs":["Draw a reference architecture for a three-tier web application and annotate where each secure design principle (least privilege, defense in depth, fail securely, zero trust) is applied.","Using OpenSSL, generate an RSA key pair, sign a file, verify the signature, then encrypt a file with AES-256; write down which algorithm gave confidentiality, integrity and non-repudiation.","Walk a building you use and write a physical security assessment: perimeter, entry controls, server room, fire suppression and power, with three recommended improvements."]},{"id":4,"name":"Communication & network security","w":13,"topics":["OSI and TCP/IP models and where controls apply","IPv4/IPv6, secure protocols (TLS, IPsec, SSH, SNMPv3) and their uses","Converged protocols: iSCSI, VoIP, InfiniBand, Fibre Channel over Ethernet","Micro-segmentation, SDN, VXLAN, VPC and software-defined perimeters","Wireless networks: Wi-Fi security (WPA3), Bluetooth, Zigbee, cellular/5G","Content distribution networks and traffic flows (north-south, east-west)","Network components: firewalls, IDS/IPS, NAC, proxies, transmission media, endpoint security","Secure communication channels: voice, video, remote access, data communications, third-party connectivity","Network attacks and mitigations: DDoS, spoofing, on-path, DNS attacks","Monitoring and management: network observability, capacity, logging"],"notes":["Outline 4.1–4.3"],"labs":["Capture traffic with Wireshark while loading an HTTP and an HTTPS page; identify the TCP handshake and TLS handshake and map each observed protocol to its OSI layer.","Design a segmented network for a small hospital (guest Wi-Fi, clinical devices, corporate, servers) and write the firewall rule set between segments using deny-by-default.","Configure a WireGuard or OpenVPN tunnel between two VMs and document which security services (confidentiality, integrity, authentication) it provides."]},{"id":5,"name":"Identity & access management","w":13,"topics":["Controlling physical and logical access to information, systems, devices, facilities and applications","Identification, authentication and authorization; MFA and passwordless","Identity management implementation: groups, roles, AAA, session management, registration and proofing","Federated identity with third parties: SAML, OAuth 2.0, OIDC","Credential management systems and single sign-on","Just-in-time access and privileged access management","Authorization mechanisms: RBAC, rule-based, MAC, DAC, ABAC, risk-based","Identity and access provisioning lifecycle: account access review, provisioning and deprovisioning","Authentication systems: Kerberos, RADIUS, TACACS+","Access control attacks and biometrics: FAR, FRR, CER"],"notes":["Outline 5.1–5.6"],"labs":["Write a joiner-mover-leaver procedure for an organization: who approves access, how roles are assigned, when reviews happen, and how accounts are disabled on termination.","Diagram a SAML SSO login and an OIDC login step by step, labeling the identity provider, service provider/relying party, assertion or token, and where trust is established.","Build a small RBAC matrix for a clinic (reception, nurse, doctor, billing, IT) against five resources, then identify one separation-of-duties conflict and fix it."]},{"id":6,"name":"Security assessment & testing","w":12,"topics":["Designing and validating assessment, test and audit strategies (internal, external, third party)","Vulnerability assessment and penetration testing (rules of engagement, testing knowledge levels)","Log reviews, synthetic transactions and breach and attack simulation","Code review and testing: static, dynamic, fuzzing, misuse case, test coverage, interface testing","Compliance checks","Collecting security process data: account management, management review, KPIs and KRIs, backup verification, training, DR/BC","Analyzing test output and generating reports; exception handling and remediation","Conducting or facilitating security audits: SOC 1/SOC 2/SOC 3, Type I vs Type II","Location of audits: on premises, cloud, hybrid"],"notes":["Outline 6.1–6.5"],"labs":["Write rules of engagement for an external penetration test: scope, excluded systems, test window, contacts, data handling and written authorization.","Run an authenticated vs unauthenticated scan (OpenVAS or Nessus Essentials) against a lab VM and write a one-page report prioritizing three findings for management.","Define five security KPIs and three KRIs for a mid-size company, with a data source and target threshold for each."]},{"id":7,"name":"Security operations","w":13,"topics":["Investigations: evidence collection and handling, chain of custody, digital forensics tools and techniques","Logging and monitoring: SIEM, SOAR, continuous monitoring, UEBA, threat intelligence and hunting","Configuration management: provisioning, baselining, automation","Foundational operations concepts: need to know, least privilege, separation of duties, job rotation, SLAs","Resource protection: media management, backups","Incident management: detection, response, mitigation, reporting, recovery, remediation, lessons learned","Detective and preventive measures: firewalls, IDS/IPS, allow and deny lists, sandboxing, honeypots, anti-malware, ML/AI tools","Patch and vulnerability management; change management","Recovery strategies: backup types, recovery sites, resilience, high availability","Disaster recovery processes and DR plan testing (read-through, walkthrough, simulation, parallel, full interruption)","Business continuity participation, physical security and personnel safety (travel, duress, emergency management)"],"notes":["Outline 7.1–7.15"],"labs":["Write an incident response playbook for ransomware on one file server, covering each phase from detection through lessons learned, with named roles and escalation points.","Collect a forensic image of a lab VM disk with dd or FTK Imager, hash it before and after, and fill in a chain-of-custody form.","Plan a tabletop DR exercise: pick a scenario, set objectives, list participants and injects, and define how you will measure whether RTOs would be met."]},{"id":8,"name":"Software development security","w":10,"topics":["Security in the SDLC: waterfall, agile, DevOps, DevSecOps, scaled agile","Maturity models: CMM, SAMM; operations, maintenance and change management","Integrated product teams and security in the development ecosystem","Development ecosystem controls: languages, libraries, toolsets, IDE, runtime, CI/CD, SCM, code repositories","Application security testing: SAST, DAST, SCA, IAST","Assessing effectiveness of software security: auditing, logging, risk analysis","Security impact of acquired software: COTS, open source, third party, managed services (SaaS, PaaS, IaaS)","Secure coding guidelines and standards: source code weaknesses, API security, secure coding practices","Software-defined security","Common weaknesses: injection, XSS, CSRF, buffer overflow, race conditions, insecure deserialization"],"notes":["Outline 8.1–8.5"],"labs":["Threat-model a small web app (login, profile, payments) with STRIDE: draw the data flow diagram, mark trust boundaries, and list one threat and mitigation per STRIDE category.","Run a SAST tool (Semgrep) and an SCA tool (npm audit or OWASP Dependency-Check) on an open-source project; triage the top five findings as a manager would.","Write security gates for a CI/CD pipeline: what must pass at commit, build, test and release, and who can approve an exception."]}],"study":{"1":[["A business unit wants to launch a product that carries risk above the organization's appetite. What is the security professional's role?","Advise, not decide. Quantify and explain the risk and options (mitigate, transfer, avoid, accept) to senior management; the business owner makes and documents the decision."],["Explain due care vs due diligence with an example.","Due diligence is doing the research to know what is needed (assessing a vendor's controls). Due care is acting on it, doing what a reasonable person would (requiring encryption in the contract and monitoring it)."],["Order the ISC2 Code of Ethics canons and say why the order matters.","1) Protect society, the common good and the infrastructure; 2) act honorably, honestly, justly, responsibly and legally; 3) provide diligent and competent service to principals; 4) advance and protect the profession. When canons conflict, the earlier one wins."],["What is the first thing to do when building a BCP, and why?","Get senior management support and define scope, then run a BIA. Without management backing there is no budget or authority; the BIA identifies critical processes and sets MTD, RTO and RPO."],["When would you choose qualitative over quantitative risk analysis?","When reliable figures for asset value and frequency are not available, or you need a fast prioritization. Quantitative (SLE, ALE) is better for cost-justifying controls when data exists."],["How do you measure whether security awareness training works?","Tie it to outcomes: phishing simulation click and report rates over time, incidents caused by user error, and policy violations, not just completion rates."]],"2":[["Who is ultimately accountable for data classification, and who implements the protection?","The data owner (a senior business manager) is accountable and assigns the classification. The custodian (often IT) implements the controls the owner specifies."],["Why classify data at all?","So protection is proportional to value and sensitivity. It avoids overspending on low-value data and underprotecting critical data, and it drives handling, retention and disposal rules."],["An organization is retiring SSDs that held regulated data. What is the best sanitization approach?","Overwriting is unreliable on SSDs due to wear leveling. Use cryptographic erase or the manufacturer's secure erase (purge), or physical destruction when the data is highly sensitive."],["What is the difference between scoping and tailoring a baseline?","Scoping removes controls that do not apply to the system. Tailoring adjusts the remaining controls (parameters, compensating controls) to fit the organization's mission."],["Why is keeping data longer than needed a risk?","It increases breach impact, legal discovery exposure and storage costs, and may violate privacy law. Retention should follow a documented schedule and legal holds."]],"3":[["Contrast Bell-LaPadula and Biba in one sentence each.","Bell-LaPadula protects confidentiality: no read up, no write down. Biba protects integrity: no read down, no write up."],["Why do we say 'fail securely' and when might a business choose fail-open instead?","Systems should default to a secure state on failure (deny access). Where life safety is at stake, such as fire doors, fail-safe/open is chosen because people come first."],["How would you explain the key management lifecycle to a manager?","Keys are generated, distributed, stored, used, rotated, revoked/archived and destroyed. Strong algorithms are useless if keys are poorly stored or never rotated."],["What security concerns are specific to ICS/OT?","Availability and safety come first, systems are old and hard to patch, protocols lack authentication. Use segmentation, strict remote access, monitoring and compensating controls."],["Which fire suppression system is usually best for a data center and why?","Pre-action water sprinklers are common because they need two triggers before water flows, reducing accidental discharge; clean-agent gas systems protect equipment but cost more."],["Why is 'security by design' cheaper than bolting it on later?","Fixing a flaw in requirements or design costs far less than in production, and design-level controls are more effective and harder to bypass."]],"4":[["Why does micro-segmentation matter in a zero trust architecture?","It limits east-west movement: each workload only talks to what it needs, so a compromised host cannot reach the rest of the network."],["What is the manager-level answer to 'which VPN should we use'?","Start from requirements: who connects, what data, what assurance level. Then choose (IPsec site-to-site, TLS/SSL client VPN, or ZTNA) and ensure strong authentication and logging."],["What is the difference between IDS and IPS in terms of business risk?","IDS only alerts, so it cannot disrupt business but also does not stop attacks. IPS blocks inline, stopping attacks but risking false-positive outages."],["How do you protect against DDoS at the organizational level?","Capacity planning, an upstream scrubbing or CDN provider, rate limiting, and a tested DDoS response plan with the ISP; you cannot solve volumetric attacks alone on premises."],["Why is WPA3-Enterprise preferred for corporate Wi-Fi?","It uses 802.1X with per-user authentication and individual keys, so credentials can be revoked per person and traffic cannot be decrypted with a shared passphrase."]],"5":[["What is the most important control in the identity lifecycle?","Timely deprovisioning and periodic access reviews. Accounts that outlive the need (terminated users, role changes) cause privilege creep and orphaned access."],["Compare SAML and OIDC.","Both enable federated SSO. SAML uses XML assertions and suits enterprise web apps; OIDC is an identity layer on OAuth 2.0 using JWTs and suits modern web and mobile apps."],["Why is OAuth alone not authentication?","OAuth 2.0 is an authorization framework that grants delegated access tokens; it does not by itself tell the app who the user is. OIDC adds that identity layer."],["What does the crossover error rate tell you about a biometric system?","The point where FAR equals FRR. A lower CER means a more accurate system and is the standard way to compare devices."],["Why use just-in-time privileged access?","Standing admin rights are an attacker's target. JIT grants elevated rights only when approved and for a limited time, with logging, reducing exposure."],["Which access control model fits a military classification system, and which fits a fast-changing company?","MAC with labels and clearances fits the military. RBAC (or ABAC for fine-grained, context-aware decisions) fits most businesses."]],"6":[["What must exist before any penetration test starts?","Written authorization from someone with authority over the systems, plus agreed scope and rules of engagement. Without it, testing may be illegal."],["SOC 2 Type I vs Type II: which gives more assurance and why?","Type II, because it tests operating effectiveness of controls over a period (typically 6–12 months). Type I only describes design at a point in time."],["Why use synthetic transactions?","They proactively test application availability and performance by scripting user actions, detecting problems before real users do, unlike passive real user monitoring."],["What makes a good security test report for management?","An executive summary in business terms, risk-rated findings, clear remediation owners and timelines, and trends against previous assessments."],["Why are backup verification data a security metric?","Backups that cannot be restored are worthless. Regular restore tests prove recovery objectives can actually be met."]],"7":[["What is the first priority in any incident or disaster?","Human safety. After that, contain the damage and preserve evidence, following the IR plan."],["Why does chain of custody matter even if you never plan to go to court?","Incidents can turn into legal matters later. Documented handling proves evidence was not altered, keeping options open."],["Compare hot, warm and cold sites.","Hot: fully equipped with current data, fastest recovery, highest cost. Warm: hardware ready but data must be restored. Cold: space and power only, cheapest but slowest."],["Which DR test is most realistic, and why is it rarely done?","Full interruption, because it actually shuts down primary operations. It risks causing a real outage, so parallel tests are usually preferred."],["Why is job rotation a security control?","It deters and detects fraud because another person performs the duties and may spot irregularities; it also reduces key-person dependency."],["What makes a lessons-learned meeting effective?","Held soon after the incident, blameless, focused on root cause, and producing tracked actions that update plans, controls and training."]],"8":[["When is the cheapest time to fix a security flaw in software?","During requirements and design. Security should be built in from the start of the SDLC, not tested in at the end."],["Why include software composition analysis (SCA) in the pipeline?","Most code is third-party libraries. SCA finds known-vulnerable and badly licensed components so they can be updated before release."],["What is the manager's concern when acquiring COTS or SaaS software?","Vendor security assurance: contract terms, certifications (SOC 2, ISO 27001), patch commitments, data location, escrow or exit strategy, and right to audit."],["Why separate development, test and production environments?","It prevents untested code and developers from affecting production and keeps production data out of less-protected environments; it supports separation of duties."],["What is the best defense against SQL injection?","Parameterized queries (prepared statements) with input validation and least-privileged database accounts; WAFs help but are compensating, not primary."],["What does a maturity model like SAMM give an organization?","A way to measure the current state of software security practices, set target levels and build a roadmap to improve over time."]]},"qCount":150,"hasLessons":true,"hasLessonsEs":true,"hasPbqs":true,"hasLevels":true});
